\section{Generated bottleneck processes}
\label{app:bottleneck-family}

\cref{sec:exp-didactic} measures the action gap on five bottleneck processes drawn at random, so that its conclusions do not rest on one hand-drawn graph.
This appendix gives the generator, the sampled processes, the population optima that the main text omits, and the per-process curves.

\subsection{Generator}
\label{app:bottleneck-family-gen}

Each process has nine states, so that the rank axis has the same meaning in all of them, and is drawn as follows.
The states are split into a first group of three to five states, one cut state, and a second group holding the remaining three to five states.
Every state has two actions and every action leads deterministically to one state.
Inside the first group, the first action of each state follows a random ordering of that group, and the last state of the ordering reaches the cut; the second action goes to a random other state of the same group.
The first action of the cut state enters a random state of the second group and its second action falls back into the first group; this is the only edge from the first group to the second, and no edge returns, so every path to the goal passes through the cut.
Inside the second group, which is closed, the first action of each state follows a random ordering whose last state is the goal; the state before it is the decision state, whose first action enters the goal and whose second action goes to a random other state of the group that is not the goal.
The fixed continuation policy is not uniform: the probability of the first action is drawn independently per state from $\mathcal U(0.25,0.75)$, so the action probabilities differ across states and across processes.
A draw is kept only if the exact action gap at its decision state is at least $0.5$, so that the decision is a real one.

Everything else matches the hand-drawn process: discount $0.8$, reward $1$ on arrival at the goal, uniform reference weight $1/n$ over candidate states, exact population expectations, ranks $2$ to $9$, closed-form fits and TD-trained fits with a bootstrapped target network.
The five processes are shown in \cref{fig:family-graphs}; their group sizes range from $3/5$ to $5/3$ and their exact action gaps from $0.77$ to $1.22$.

