    \end{subfigure}
    \caption{
    \textbf{The five generated processes.}
    Solid arrows are the first action, dashed arrows the second; the number next to each state is the probability its fixed policy assigns to the first action.
    The orange state is the one-way cut: its first action is the only edge into the second group and no edge returns.
    The bottleneck is the decision at the green-outlined state, whose two actions lead to different futures for the goal reward: its first action enters the goal (filled green) on the next step, its second returns into the group and reaches the goal only later.
    The action gap is measured at that state-action pair; in every process the cut precedes the decision.
    }
    \label{fig:family-graphs}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Population optima and per-process results}
\label{app:bottleneck-family-result}

\Cref{fig:family-ablation-closed} shows the six arms at their population optimum, the counterpart of the TD-trained panel in \cref{fig:toy-ablation}.
Fitting the normalized target is identical to FB by construction and its curve coincides at every rank.
Both softmax arms retain at least $0.85$ of the gap at every rank, including rank $2$; FB's own optimum retains $0.09$, $0.22$ and $0.49$ at ranks $2$, $3$ and $4$, so on generated processes its failure is not confined to the bootstrapped fixed point as it is on the hand-drawn process.
Reading the softmax model through FB's linear shortcut fails at the optimum as well, and the soft immediate target is a ceiling that does not move with rank.

\Cref{tab:family-rank} reports, for each process, the smallest rank at which each arm recovers $90\%$ of that process's exact action gap; \cref{fig:family-per-mdp} shows the per-process curves behind it.
The ordering of the arms is the same on every process.
Every arm reconstructs at least $84\%$ of the squared mass of the successor matrix at every rank, so the differences are invisible in aggregate reconstruction error.

