\subsection{The rare-bottleneck measurement in full}
\label{app:rare-bottleneck-full}

\Cref{fig:rare-bottleneck} shows one panel of the measurement of \cref{lem:rare-bottleneck}.
\Cref{fig:rare-bottleneck-full} adds the other two: the objective cost of a rank-$3$ fit that keeps the gap, which converges to about a fifth of the optimum rather than vanishing, and the same gap fraction as ordinary states are added with uniform weights instead of reweighting the bottleneck rows.
What disappears as the bottleneck becomes rare is the incentive to pay for it, not the cost.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/rare_bottleneck/fig_rare_bottleneck.pdf}
    \caption{
    \textbf{A rare bottleneck under squared reconstruction, all three measurements.}
    Rank $3$ on the process of \cref{fig:didactic-mdp}.
    Left: fraction of the exact action gap preserved as the weight of the two bottleneck rows decreases (lines: optimum; hollow markers: TD-trained).
    Middle: those rows' share of the squared objective, and the relative objective cost of a rank-$3$ FB fit that keeps the gap.
    Right: the same gap fraction as ordinary states are added with uniform weights.
    }
    \label{fig:rare-bottleneck-full}
\end{figure}
